Nrityapath AI

AI-powered Bharatanatyam movement & storytelling notation generator

Nrityapath uses AI to convert movement descriptions or narrative stories into structured Bharatanatyam choreography notation — complete with posture glyphs, mudras, beats, expression cues, and teaching notes.

It introduces a brand-new, lightweight notation standard called NNF (Nrityapath Notation Format) for classical dance.

⸻

What is Nrityapath?

Bharatanatyam has traditionally been transmitted orally, visually, and physically — but never through a modern digital notation system.

Nrityapath bridges that gap.

Using AI, it transforms text inputs into clear, structured choreography sequences suitable for:
	•	learning
	•	teaching
	•	documenting
	•	choreographing
	•	storytelling

It works in two modes:

1️. Movement → Notation

Short phrases describing posture, mudra, direction, and beats.

2️. Story → Notation

Narrative inputs describing characters, emotions, or scenarios.

Both modes output standardized JSON following the NNF schema.

⸻

Who Is It For?

Primary Users
	•	Dancers – beginners to advanced
	•	Choreographers – documenting or ideating sequences
	•	Students – learning adavus or expressive movement

Secondary Users
	•	Teachers / Gurus
	•	Movement storytellers
	•	Anyone exploring classical arts through digital tools

⸻

Key Features

✔ Text → Structured Dance Notation

Convert movement cues or adavus into step-by-step instructions.

✔ Story → Expressive Choreography

Translate emotions and narratives into dance sequences with rasa.

✔ Stick-Figure / Mudra Visualization (Framer UI)

Each JSON step can be rendered as:
	•	posture glyph
	•	hand mudra icon
	•	directional arrow
	•	beat timing
	•	expression cues
	•	guru-style teaching notes

✔ Designed for AI Genesis Hackathon

Works seamlessly with:
	•	Gemini (prompting)
	•	Opus (workflow + audit)
	•	Framer (UI/visualization)

⸻

NNF — Nrityapath Notation Format

The specification lives in:
nnf_schema.md

It defines:
	•	postures (ARA, SAM, MND)
	•	mudras (ALA, KAT, TRI, PTA)
	•	movement types (FWD, TURN_R, STATIC, etc.)
	•	beats (1B, 2B, 4B)
	•	direction
	•	rasa expression
	•	teaching cues

Output Structure (simplified)
{
  "mode": "movement",
  "level": "beginner",
  "rasa": "none",
  "steps": [
      { /* step object */ }
  ]
}

